Here is the R code we used to find confidence intervals and hypothesis testing for our question of interest. 

```{r setup, include=FALSE}
knitr::opts_chunk$set(echo = TRUE)
```

Code to import packages and data and ensure data is properly imported
```{r, message = FALSE}
library(readr)
library(dplyr)
library(ggplot2)
library(infer)

set.seed(6967420)

data <- read_csv("project2_data.csv")
```

This makes the object "data" just show the variables we are interested in, and takes out all NA responses
```{r}
data <- data |> select("AGE", "SEX", "SEXORIEN", "DEPRESSEV", "ANXIETYEV", "ECIGSTATUS") |> 
  na.exclude()
```

This changes the ECIGSTATUS responses to "user" and "nonuser" instead of "Current", "Former", and "Never", Just to consolidate people who have ever used e-cigarettes.

```{r}
data$ECIGSTATUS <- ifelse(data$ECIGSTATUS %in% c("Current", "Former"), "user", "nonuser")
```

This changes the SEXORIEN responses to "queer" and "straight" instead of "Gay/Lesbian", "Bisexual", "Something else", or "Straight", again to consolidate our data into 2 groups we can compare.

```{r}
data$SEXORIEN <- ifelse(data$SEXORIEN %in% c("Something else", "Bisexual", "Gay/Lesbian"),"queer","straight")
```

CI for The difference in e-cigarette use between straight and queer individuals,
using percentile method.

Here we are filtering our data to make it more usable for this section, then creating a bootstrap distribution for the ecig usage and creating a confidence interval.

```{r}
ecig_data <- data |> 
  select("SEXORIEN","ECIGSTATUS")

summarize(data, 
          mean(ECIGSTATUS == "user"),
          .by = "SEXORIEN")
ecig_phat = 0.368 - 0.161
ecig_phat

ecig_boot <- ecig_data |> 
  specify(formula = ECIGSTATUS ~ SEXORIEN, success = "user") |> 
  generate(reps = 1000, type = "bootstrap") |> 
  calculate(stat = "diff in props", order = c("queer", "straight"))

```

Our point estimate is 0.207.
We want to use a confidence interval rather than just our point estimate because it gives us more information about where the true population proportion could lie. Additionally, we are using a 95% confidence level.

```{r}
ecig_ci <- ecig_boot |> 
  get_ci(level = 0.95, type = "percentile")
ecig_ci
visualize(ecig_boot) +
  shade_ci(ecig_ci)
```

Our bootstrap distribution is symmetrical and normally distributed. We can use this graph to show our confidence interval.

Both endpoints are positive, so we are 95% confident that the population proportion of e-cigarette users is between 0.180 and 0.236 percentage points higher for queer individuals than for straight individuals.


Hypothesis Testing:



We are using two sets of hypotheses:
Null Hypothesis: There is no difference in anxiety levels between queer and straight people
Alternative Hypothesis: There are higher levels of anxiety in queer people than straight people
Null Hypothesis: There is no difference in depression levels between queer and straight people
Alternative Hypothesis: There are higher levels of depression in queer people than straight people

Additionally, we are using an $\alpha$ of 0.05.

Hypothesis testing code for anxiety:

```{r}
ggplot(data = data) +
  geom_bar(aes(x = SEXORIEN, fill = ANXIETYEV), position = "fill") +
  xlab("Sexual Orientation") +
  ylab("Proportion")

anxiety_diff <- data |> 
  specify(formula = ANXIETYEV ~ SEXORIEN, success = "Yes") |>
  hypothesize(null = "independence") |> 
  generate(reps = 1000, type = "permute") |> 
  calculate(stat = "diff in props", order = c("queer", "straight"))
anxiety_phat <- mean(data$ANXIETYEV == "Yes")
visualize(anxiety_diff) +
  shade_pvalue(obs_stat = anxiety_phat, direction = "right")
```



Hypothesis testing code for depression:
```{r}
ggplot(data = data) +
  geom_bar(aes(x = SEXORIEN, fill = DEPRESSEV), position = "fill") +
  xlab("Sexual Orientation") +
  ylab("Proportion")

depression_diff <- data |> 
  specify(formula = DEPRESSEV ~ SEXORIEN, success = "Yes") |>
  hypothesize(null = "independence") |> 
  generate(reps = 1000, type = "permute") |> 
  calculate(stat = "diff in props", order = c("queer", "straight"))
depression_phat <- mean(data$DEPRESSEV == "Yes")
visualize(depression_diff) +
  shade_pvalue(obs_stat = depression_phat, direction = "right")

```

This calculates the p-value for both the anxiety and depression hypothesis tests and creates our visualizations.

```{r}
anxiety_pvalue <- anxiety_diff |> 
  get_pvalue(obs_stat = anxiety_phat,
             direction = "right")

depression_pvalue <- depression_diff |> 
  get_pvalue(obs_stat = depression_phat,
             direction = "right")
anxiety_pvalue
depression_pvalue
```

P value interpretations:
We would have less than a 0.0001% chance of getting the results we did under the null circumstances for both hypotheses.

Formal conclusion for anxiety:
With a p-value of less than 0.0001, we can reject our null hypothesis. There is significant evidence that queer individuals have higher rates of anxiety than straight individuals.

Formal conclusion for depression:
With a p-value of less than 0.0001, we can reject our null hypothesis. There is significant evidence that queer individuals have higher rates of depression than straight individuals.

